<PageTitle>Tree grid — FlexCore Showcase</PageTitle>

<FeatureDemoLayout Title="Tree grid" Category="Data Grid" CategoryHref="demo/grid" ControlName="TreeGridControl"
                   Tagline="A flat list with Id and ParentId columns, shown as a collapsible hierarchy."
                   SourcePath="Grid/TreeGridControl.razor;Grid/TreeGridControl.razor.cs;Grid/TreeGridControl.Checking.cs;Grid/TreeGridControl.Editing.cs;Grid/TreeGridControl.Frozen.cs;Grid/TreeGridControl.Loading.cs;Grid/TreeGridControl.Moving.cs;Grid/TreeGridControl.Query.cs;Grid/TreeGridColumn.cs;Grid/TreeGridMutationModels.cs;Grid/TreeGridQuery.cs;Grid/GridModels.cs;Grid/GridToolbarModels.cs;Grid/GridEnums.cs">
    <Overview>
        <p>
            <code>TreeGridControl</code> builds the hierarchy from two property names on a flat list: <code>IdMapping</code> names the key and
            <code>ParentIdMapping</code> the parent key, null or empty for a root. <code>TreatAsParent</code> marks rows as expandable even
            before their children are in the list, <code>TreeColumnIndex</code> picks the column that carries the indentation and icons, <code>NodeExpandIconStyle</code> is
            <code>PlusMinus</code> or <code>Triangle</code>, <code>EnableCollapseAll</code> starts with every node collapsed unless an
            <code>InitialExpansionPredicate</code> says otherwise, and <code>ToggleOnRowClick="false"</code> limits mouse toggling to the icon; the keyboard still toggles. <code>Expanded</code> and <code>Collapsed</code> report the node
            and its level; <code>ExpandAllAsync</code> and <code>CollapseAllAsync</code> change the state from code without raising those events.
        </p>
    </Overview>

    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Id="tree" Title="Suppliers and their services" RazorCode="@Code.Tree" CSharpCode="@Code.TreeCs">
            <Description><p>Each supplier is a root row and the services it sells are its children, all in one flat list; the suppliers are named from the operator's destinations (Hotel Lisbon Central, Lisbon Walks). A service row shows its unit, net rate and bookings, a supplier row its category, service count and bookings total. Click a plus to open a supplier, or use Expand all. Change the size to rebuild the list with more suppliers and services; the line under the grid counts the rows the grid received and names the last node you opened or closed.</p></Description>
            <Controls>
                <ButtonGroupControl Items="_sizes" Values="@(new[] { _supplierCount.ToString() })"
                                    ValuesChanged="@(v => SetScale(v.Count > 0 ? int.Parse(v[0]) : 10))"
                                    Selection="ChoiceSelectionMode.Single" AriaLabel="Tree size" />
                <ButtonControl CssClass="btn-sm" OnClick="@(() => _tree?.ExpandAllAsync() ?? Task.CompletedTask)">Expand all</ButtonControl>
                <ButtonControl CssClass="btn-sm" OnClick="@(() => _tree?.CollapseAllAsync() ?? Task.CompletedTask)">Collapse all</ButtonControl>
            </Controls>
            <Demo>
                <TreeGridControl @ref="_tree"
                                 @key="_treeGeneration"
                                 TValue="SupplierNode"
                                 DataSource="@_treeNodes"
                                 IdMapping="Id"
                                 ParentIdMapping="ParentId"
                                 TreeColumnIndex="0"
                                 EnableCollapseAll="true"
                                 AllowSelection="true"
                                 ToggleOnRowClick="false"
                                 Height="440px"
                                 CssClass="fx-treegrid-compact fx-treegrid-yellow-folder fx-treegrid-dotted-lines"
                                 NodeExpandIconStyle="GroupExpandIconStyle.PlusMinus"
                                 TreatAsParent="TreatAsParent"
                                 Expanded="@(a => _last = $"opened {a.Data?.Name} (level {a.Level})")"
                                 Collapsed="@(a => _last = $"closed {a.Data?.Name} (level {a.Level})")">
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="Name" HeaderText="Supplier and service" Width="340px" />
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="Category" HeaderText="Category" Width="130px" />
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="ServiceCount" HeaderText="Services" Width="90px" TextAlign="TextAlign.Center" />
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="Unit" HeaderText="Unit" Width="70px" TextAlign="TextAlign.Center" />
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="Rate" HeaderText="Rate" Width="120px" TextAlign="TextAlign.Right" Format="$#,##0.00" />
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="Bookings" HeaderText="Bookings" Width="100px" TextAlign="TextAlign.Right" />
                </TreeGridControl>
                <p class="result">@_treeNodes.Count rows: @_supplierCount suppliers with @_servicesPerSupplier services each@(_last is null ? "" : $" · last {_last}")</p>
            </Demo>
        </DemoExample>
        <DemoExample Id="org" Title="Org chart: who reports to whom" RazorCode="@Code.Org" CSharpCode="@Code.OrgCs">
            <Description><p>Twenty-one people of an HR directory, each row carrying its manager's id. Pick a department: the people in it stay, together with the managers above them, so the tree never loses its root. Managers show how many people report to them, and the performance column is a read-only <code>RatingControl</code> inside a column template.</p></Description>
            <Controls>
                <ButtonGroupControl Items="_deptItems" Values="@(new[] { _dept })" ValuesChanged="ShowDepartment" Selection="ChoiceSelectionMode.Single" AriaLabel="Department" />
            </Controls>
            <Demo>
                <TreeGridControl @key="_orgGeneration"
                                 TValue="EmployeeRow"
                                 DataSource="@_shown"
                                 IdMapping="EmployeeID"
                                 ParentIdMapping="ReportsToID"
                                 TreeColumnIndex="0"
                                 EnableCollapseAll="true"
                                 AllowSelection="true"
                                 ToggleOnRowClick="false"
                                 InitialExpansionPredicate="@(_ => true)"
                                 Height="440px">
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="@nameof(EmployeeRow.FullName)" HeaderText="Name" Width="260px">
                        <Template>
                            @{ var person = (EmployeeRow)context; }
                            <strong>@person.FullName</strong>@(person.IsManager ? $" · manages {person.SubordinatesCount}" : "")
                        </Template>
                    </TreeGridColumn>
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="@nameof(EmployeeRow.JobTitle)" HeaderText="Title" Width="200px" />
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="@nameof(EmployeeRow.Department)" HeaderText="Department" Width="130px" />
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="@nameof(EmployeeRow.Office)" HeaderText="Office" Width="120px" />
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="@nameof(EmployeeRow.Salary)" HeaderText="Salary" Width="110px" TextAlign="TextAlign.Right" Format="C0" />
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="@nameof(EmployeeRow.Rating)" HeaderText="Performance" Width="130px">
                        <Template>
                            @{ var person = (EmployeeRow)context; }
                            <RatingControl Value="@person.Rating" ReadOnly="true" />
                        </Template>
                    </TreeGridColumn>
                    <TreeGridColumn Field="@nameof(EmployeeRow.HireDate)" HeaderText="Hired" Width="110px" TextAlign="TextAlign.Center" Format="yyyy-MM-dd" />
                </TreeGridControl>
                <p class="result">@OrgStatus</p>
            </Demo>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>

    <Vocabulary>
        <p>
            <code>TreeGridControl</code> renders its own table: it is not a <code>GridControl</code> wrapper and forwards no parameter. It shares
            <code>GridControl</code>'s best-fit engine (<code>ColumnAutoFit</code>), sort pipeline, exporter and toolbar item model, and takes its
            events as direct parameters rather than an <code>EventsRef</code> object. One row is selected at a time. The 97 parameters are
            grouped by purpose: 74 in the tables below, the 23 <code>EventCallback</code>s under Events.
        </p>
